{
  "metadata": {
    "run_id": "20260924T224146Z",
    "review_date": "2026-09-24",
    "last_updated": "2026-09-24T22:41:46.722004+00:00"
  },
  "company_name": "Cadence",
  "slug": "cadence",
  "stage": "seed",
  "sector": "B2B SaaS",
  "geography": "US",
  "sector_type": "saas"
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "structural_tam_gt_sam_gt_som",
      "category": "Structural Checks",
      "label": "TAM > SAM > SOM",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "Both approaches are properly nested. Top-down: TAM $3.1B > SAM $682M > SOM $13.6M. Bottom-up: TAM $1.224B > SAM $734M > SOM $14.7M. The hierarchy holds in both builds."
    },
    {
      "id": "structural_definitions_correct",
      "category": "Structural Checks",
      "label": "Definitions used correctly",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "TAM is used correctly as the full addressable universe (all US FSM software spend in the top-down; all HVAC/plumbing/electrical firms at full ARPU in the bottom-up). SAM is narrowed by serviceable segment. SOM is the realistic near-term capture. Definitions are applied consistently across both approaches."
    },
    {
      "id": "tam_matches_product_scope",
      "category": "TAM Scoping",
      "label": "TAM matches product scope",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "The top-down correctly starts from the US Field Service Management software market as the industry total and then narrows to the HVAC/plumbing/electrical SMB segment at SAM. The bottom-up builds TAM directly from the three target trades. Neither approach inflates TAM with unrelated verticals such as enterprise manufacturing or government; the scope is appropriate for a field-service scheduling product targeting home-service SMBs."
    },
    {
      "id": "source_segments_match",
      "category": "TAM Scoping",
      "label": "Source segments match product segments",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "The IBISWorld and Global Market Insights sources cover the US FSM software category \u2014 an exact or near match to Cadence's product category. Customer-count sources (Workyard, Sequoia Geo, NECA) are trade-specific for HVAC, plumbing, and electrical respectively. ARPU benchmarks are drawn from published FSM SaaS competitor pricing. All sources are US-focused and segment-relevant; no source references a materially broader or unrelated market."
    },
    {
      "id": "som_share_defensible",
      "category": "SOM Realism",
      "label": "SOM share is defensible",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "Both approaches apply a 2% capture rate against SAM, producing SOM figures of $13.6M (top-down) and $14.7M (bottom-up). At seed stage, 2% of a well-scoped SAM is a modest and defensible claim. It falls well within the 5% ceiling that typically requires additional justification in a broad, fragmented market. The figure is flagged as an estimate rather than sourced, which is appropriate given the company's early stage."
    },
    {
      "id": "som_backed_by_gtm",
      "category": "SOM Realism",
      "label": "SOM backed by go-to-market plan",
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "notes": "No go-to-market materials were provided \u2014 the analysis was built from a conversational description only. There is no deck slide, sales funnel, or GTM plan to assess the SOM capture rate against. This item will become scorable once customer-acquisition strategy or channel metrics are documented."
    },
    {
      "id": "som_consistent_with_projections",
      "category": "SOM Realism",
      "label": "SOM consistent with financial projections",
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "notes": "No financial model or hiring plan was provided. Without projections showing sales capacity, headcount ramp, or burn rate, there is no basis to check whether the SOM revenue figure is operationally achievable. This item will become scorable when financial projections are available."
    },
    {
      "id": "data_current",
      "category": "Data Quality",
      "label": "Data is current",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "The primary market-size figure comes from IBISWorld's 2026 US FSM market data, accessed September 2026. Trade association data (NECA 2024 electrical contractor profile) is from 2024. Competitor pricing references are from 2026. All sources fall within the last two years, which is appropriate for a B2B SaaS market sizing."
    },
    {
      "id": "sources_reputable",
      "category": "Data Quality",
      "label": "Sources are reputable",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "The market-size foundation uses IBISWorld and Global Market Insights \u2014 established analyst firms. Trade counts draw on NECA (a professional industry association), Workyard, and Sequoia Geo (industry data aggregators). ARPU is benchmarked against Housecall Pro's published pricing and an FSM pricing comparison. The ARPU source (a competitor's blog-style pricing guide) is lower-tier, but is used as a cross-check on a derived estimate, not as the sole foundation of a headline figure. The source mix is acceptable for a seed-stage analysis."
    },
    {
      "id": "figures_triangulated",
      "category": "Data Quality",
      "label": "Key figures triangulated",
      "status": "fail",
      "notes": "The top-down industry total is corroborated by two independent analyst sources (IBISWorld and Global Market Insights) and qualifies as triangulated. However, the customer count, ARPU, and segment percentage each rely on a single primary source or a chain of factors drawn from one data point per factor. ARPU in particular rests on one competitor's published pricing combined with an illustrative blended estimate \u2014 not two independent confirmations of the same figure. Sourcing a second independent ARPU benchmark (for example, a public S-1 disclosure or a second FSM vendor's pricing page for a comparable plan) would strengthen this."
    },
    {
      "id": "unsupported_figures_flagged",
      "category": "Data Quality",
      "label": "Unsupported figures flagged",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "The two agent-estimate parameters \u2014 the top-down market-share rate and the bottom-up capture rate, both set at 2% \u2014 are explicitly labeled as estimates rather than sourced figures. No unsupported number is presented as validated. Derived assumptions show their factor chains with source attributions."
    },
    {
      "id": "validated_used_precisely",
      "category": "Data Quality",
      "label": "Validated used precisely",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "The top-down industry total is recorded as validated (2 sources). The bottom-up TAM, customer count, ARPU, and segment percentage are recorded as partially supported (1 source each). No figure is claimed as validated without meeting the two-source standard. The distinction between validated and partially supported is applied correctly throughout."
    },
    {
      "id": "assumptions_categorized",
      "category": "Data Quality",
      "label": "Assumptions categorized",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "All seven quantitative assumptions carry explicit categories: industry total is sourced with a citation; segment percentage, ARPU, and serviceable percentage are derived with their factor chains shown; market-share rate and capture rate are labeled as estimates. No assumption is unlabeled or presented without a category."
    },
    {
      "id": "both_approaches_used",
      "category": "Methodology",
      "label": "Both approaches used",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "The analysis runs both a top-down build (US FSM industry total narrowed by segment and share) and an independent bottom-up build (340,000 HVAC/plumbing/electrical firms at $3,600 ARPU, narrowed by serviceability and capture rate). Both approaches are fully computed and their results are compared."
    },
    {
      "id": "approaches_reconciled",
      "category": "Methodology",
      "label": "Gap between the two approaches is explained",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "The 86.8% TAM gap between the two approaches is driven by a well-defined scope difference: the top-down starts from the full US FSM software market across all verticals, while the bottom-up is built from only the three trades Cadence targets. This difference is stated in the methodology rationale. SAM and SOM converge to within 7.4%, which the analysis correctly notes is arithmetic closeness, not independent confirmation \u2014 shared underlying inputs (the 2% capture rate applied in both approaches) are identified as a factor."
    },
    {
      "id": "growth_dynamics_considered",
      "category": "Methodology",
      "label": "Market growth dynamics considered",
      "status": "fail",
      "notes": "The analysis is scoped to current-year figures and does not include an explicit market CAGR or growth-rate assumption. One source notes that FSM software adoption is projected to grow from roughly 48% to 70% of firms by 2027, but this is embedded in the serviceability factor rather than surfaced as a standalone growth assumption. Investors will typically ask about market trajectory \u2014 citing the FSM software CAGR (available from IBISWorld or Global Market Insights) and noting whether the market is expanding would strengthen the narrative."
    },
    {
      "id": "market_properly_segmented",
      "category": "Market Understanding",
      "label": "Market properly segmented",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "SAM is derived through explicit segment logic in both approaches. The top-down applies a three-factor chain to narrow from all FSM verticals to HVAC/plumbing/electrical SMBs (home-services share of FSM, share of home services that are the target trades, SMB proportion within those trades). The bottom-up enumerates firms by trade directly. Both use segment-specific data rather than an arbitrary percentage of a headline TAM."
    },
    {
      "id": "competitive_landscape_acknowledged",
      "category": "Market Understanding",
      "label": "Competitive landscape acknowledged",
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "notes": "No deck or materials addressing competitive positioning were provided \u2014 the analysis was built from a conversational description only. Whether Cadence's SOM capture rate accounts for established competitors such as Housecall Pro, Jobber, or ServiceTitan cannot be assessed from the materials provided. Adding a competitive positioning section when the deck is developed will allow this to be scored."
    },
    {
      "id": "sam_expansion_path_noted",
      "category": "Market Understanding",
      "label": "SAM expansion path noted",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "The target segments explicitly include other home-service SMBs as a potential SAM expansion beyond the three core trades (HVAC, plumbing, electrical). This acknowledges that the current SAM is a beachhead rather than the full addressable opportunity, which is the kind of strategic framing investors look for."
    },
    {
      "id": "assumptions_explicit",
      "category": "Presentation",
      "label": "Assumptions explicit",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "Every key assumption \u2014 industry total, segment narrowing percentage, market-share estimate, contractor firm count, annual revenue per firm, serviceability rate, and capture rate \u2014 is stated explicitly with its value, category, and either a source citation or a factor breakdown. An investor can challenge any individual input without reverse-engineering the model."
    },
    {
      "id": "formulas_shown",
      "category": "Presentation",
      "label": "Formulas shown",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "Every figure shows the formula behind it: top-down SAM = TAM \u00d7 segment percentage; top-down SOM = SAM \u00d7 share percentage; bottom-up TAM = firm count \u00d7 annual revenue per firm; bottom-up SAM = serviceable firms \u00d7 annual revenue per firm; bottom-up SOM = target firms \u00d7 annual revenue per firm. The derivation of segment percentage and ARPU also shows the factor chains with intermediate values."
    },
    {
      "id": "sources_cited",
      "category": "Presentation",
      "label": "Sources cited",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "Every externally sourced or derived figure carries at least one citation. IBISWorld and Global Market Insights are cited for the industry total; Workyard, Sequoia Geo, and NECA for trade firm counts; fieldservicesoftware.io for adoption and serviceability data; and Housecall Pro and Fieldproxy's pricing comparison for ARPU benchmarks. Source URLs, publisher names, and access dates are recorded."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "total": 22,
    "pass": 17,
    "fail": 2,
    "not_applicable": 3,
    "score_pct": 89.5,
    "overall_status": "strong",
